WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our next guest is
a very funny comedian,

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.667
actor and author,
you can see him on the road

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:05.868
touring around the country.

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:07.300
For ticket information
go to colinquinn.com.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:08.467
Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:10.868
our very good friend
Colin Quinn everybody.

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:13.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.300
♪♪

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.501
♪♪

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:25.100
Welcome back.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.734
You are one of our modern
historians

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:28.367
-Whoa!
-...of this great city.

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:29.434
-Yes.

00:00:29.434 --> 00:00:30.434
-You know a lot
about New York City.

00:00:30.434 --> 00:00:31.467
And you have a great new...

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.701
-You're coming in hot,
by the way.

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:34.400
-I'm coming in hot.
I want to big you up.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.033
-You've been on already.
You've been on already.

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:37.033
I just got out here.

00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.033
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.267
And by the way,
before we start

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:41.901
not to break the momentum,

00:00:41.901 --> 00:00:43.634
I don't like this.
-Okay.

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:45.367
-The old days of a nice cup.
-Yeah.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.200
-Look at this.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. Sorry.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.000
-Jesus.
-You really?

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.934
-Because it makes me
look cheap now

00:00:50.934 --> 00:00:51.934
that I'm drinking it.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:52.934
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.934 --> 00:00:53.968
The crowd doesn't know.

00:00:53.968 --> 00:00:55.200
They'll be like,
Look at that chiseler.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.801
People on TV,
it's all about perception.

00:00:57.801 --> 00:00:59.234
You know what I mean,
folks?

00:00:59.234 --> 00:01:00.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:03.000
I like to work the crowd.
Work in comedy is big now.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.167
So I try to work
the crowd on TV.

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:07.968
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:08.968
-Oh, great.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:11.901
-Oh.
-That should be --

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:14.167
That should be your new
catchphrase as a comedian.

00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:15.634
It isn't like
an actual catchphrase,

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:16.801
but after your punch lines,

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:18.767
you take a sip
from a tiny thing of water.

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:20.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:22.167
It gave them the exact right
amount of time to laugh.

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:23.400
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.934
-It might look a little
creepy, but you're right.

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:26.300
-Just tiny sips.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.534
And the whole thing
is, he makes it --

00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:28.901
You know,
he makes it last the whole hour.

00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:29.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:31.968
Tiny little sips.
You have a --

00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:33.968
I was -- I was setting you up.
Yes, I know.

00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:35.234
I know.
You were talk--

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:36.267
You have
this new show called --

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:37.300
-And you're like, "Hey."
-Hey.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.400
Look, you want
to talk about

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.901
"Block by Block,"
no problem.

00:01:39.901 --> 00:01:41.300
You have a YouTube
show called "Block by Block"

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.133
where you're going
to different neighborhoods

00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:44.234
in New York City.
-Yeah.

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:45.734
-And you're talking
about the history.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:47.000
What makes them unique.
-Yes.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.033
You've done Yorkville.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:49.601
You've done Hell's Kitchen.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:51.767
When you were growing up
in New York,

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:53.968
did different neighborhoods
have different reputations

00:01:53.968 --> 00:01:55.167
that were interesting to you?

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.634
-Oh, sure.
But you can't discuss those now.

00:01:57.634 --> 00:01:59.133
They're all based on ethnicity.

00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:02.000
-Okay, gotcha. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.367
So now you just are like,
"Oh, that --

00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:04.767
Oh, I know that one.
That's northeast of here.

00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:06.067
-You're like,
Oh, that block, yeah.

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:07.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:09.067
You have to get
very vague.

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:11.100
But, um, yeah.
But these guys, you know,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.300
these people
from back in the day.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.868
So my friend Steve Kelly
in Yorkville

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:16.634
and he goes over the history,

00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:18.234
but also the brawls --
the giant fights

00:02:18.234 --> 00:02:20.467
they'd have at
the Second Avenue bars.

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:23.734
He left his comic strip post
to go across the street,

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:25.734
got in a big brawl.
The cops are everywhere.

00:02:25.734 --> 00:02:27.434
And he goes back
to the comic strip

00:02:27.434 --> 00:02:30.067
where, ironically,
he's in charge of security.

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:32.234
And then my friend Mike Spillane

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:33.667
was was telling the story
about him

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:35.701
and his brother and his cousin
and a couple of other guys.

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:38.534
And they get in a fight
in a bar, a couple of guys,

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:40.234
but then he realize
one of the guy's friends

00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:41.901
they're fighting
is Andre the Giant.

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:43.534
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:45.667
So he picks him up
and just flings him.

00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:47.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:48.767
-I feel like you should get
a visual

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:50.734
on Andre the Giant
before the fight starts.

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:51.767
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:52.901
No, actually,
he did see him

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.334
in the bathroom
and he goes, "Oh, wow."

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:55.634
But he didn't know he
was with the guy...

00:02:55.634 --> 00:02:57.100
-Oh, that's the problem.
-...boys were about the fight.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.667
-I think just out of safety,
if Andre the Giant was there,

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:00.601
I wouldn't start any fights.

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:02.467
You never know
who's his plus one.

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:03.801
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:06.601
-Yes. Andre the Giant
with a plus one. I like that.

00:03:06.601 --> 00:03:07.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:09.734
-And did you -- What were, like,
what were sort of like --

00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:10.868
What about Flushing?

00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:12.601
What was the reputation
about Flushing.

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.701
-Flushing, back then
it was kind of Irish, Italian,

00:03:14.701 --> 00:03:16.501
Jewish, like a lot of names,
and now, it's all Asian.

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:19.501
And then it became Korean
and then it became Chinese.

00:03:19.501 --> 00:03:21.601
So even the Koreans
now have left Flushing.

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.267
They moved to Bayside.

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:24.968
-So it's just like it
just rotates through.

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:26.567
You just have like one
after the other,

00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:27.834
a different identity comes
through.

00:03:27.834 --> 00:03:29.467
-Yeah.
-That's fascinating.

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:30.934
-I agree.

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:33.834
-You talked about small talk
and how it's a lost art,

00:03:33.834 --> 00:03:35.234
but I was wondering,

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:36.934
small talk
in different countries,

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:38.601
you travel a lot,
do you find like

00:03:38.601 --> 00:03:40.968
certain cultures
are better at small talk?

00:03:40.968 --> 00:03:42.467
-Well, I mean Irish.

00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:44.367
It depends what you like
for small talk,

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:47.667
if you like being -- having,
you know, your balls busted,

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:49.100
The Irish people.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.100
-Yeah.
-You know.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.300
They like that banter.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.367
-Yeah.
-Is what I mean.

00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:53.400
You know what I mean?

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.767
So I was in Ireland,
like we went,

00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:57.534
we went last summer
to Ireland.

00:03:57.534 --> 00:03:59.767
So I go to the -- There's
a girl behind this, like,

00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:01.567
podium at the hotel restaurant.

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:03.634
So I'm the guy that likes to,
you know,

00:04:03.634 --> 00:04:06.200
like, I like to play around
with all the people,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.601
Like I'm the guy in my building
if somebody's mopping,

00:04:08.601 --> 00:04:10.634
every time
I walk in the lobby,

00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:12.667
I pretend to slip like,
"Hey!" and pretend.

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:13.667
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:15.000
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.901
-It's not the Mark Twain
humor prize,

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:18.567
but, you know, that's...

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:19.601
That's my thing.

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:20.801
I like to be like,
"Hey!"

00:04:20.801 --> 00:04:22.367
Have a couple
of laughs and move on.

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:24.501
Nobody gets hurt.
We'll, have a nice laugh.

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:26.234
And, um...

00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:28.033
I'm getting mad thinking about
people judging.

00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:29.033
And...

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:31.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.534 --> 00:04:33.868
Like, "Oh, yeah,
it's funny."

00:04:33.868 --> 00:04:35.834
You know, I'm not --
I'm not a prideful guy, but --

00:04:35.834 --> 00:04:37.501
-Everybody thinks it's funny
that you do that.

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:39.000
I'm sure the guy
mopping must love it.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.868
-He loves it.
-Yeah.

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:42.033
-Every time.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:43.467
And sometimes, like,
if it's the same guy,

00:04:43.467 --> 00:04:44.834
then I'll wait
the extra second

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:46.901
because, you know, surprise
is the element in comedy.

00:04:46.901 --> 00:04:48.200
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.300
And then he's like,
"Oh, he's not going to do it."

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.734
And I pass him,
then I do it anyway.

00:04:51.734 --> 00:04:53.067
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:54.334
But anyway.

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:55.434
-Ireland.
-Audience will want --

00:04:55.434 --> 00:04:56.434
Oh, so I go to Ireland.

00:04:56.434 --> 00:04:57.901
So there's a girl
behind the counter

00:04:57.901 --> 00:05:00.234
at the podium at this
restaurant in the hotel,

00:05:00.234 --> 00:05:01.934
and she's quiet, like 19.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:03.267
But Irish people,
they all have the same

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:05.300
ballbuster sense of humor.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.100
So I go, "I want the best table
in here," you know?

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.667
And she goes --
She doesn't know me --

00:05:09.667 --> 00:05:11.267
She goes,
"You deserve no less."

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:12.634
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:14.834
And I go,
"My wife is joining me."

00:05:14.834 --> 00:05:16.534
She goes,
"She's a lucky woman."

00:05:16.534 --> 00:05:19.100
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.100
I love it.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.634
-You -- you
traveled to Greece.

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:22.968
You were in Greece this summer.

00:05:22.968 --> 00:05:24.234
-I was in Greece
this summer.

00:05:24.234 --> 00:05:25.667
-What is your takeaway?
-Epiphany.

00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:27.200
I don't think there's --
Maybe that's a Greek word.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.267
-I think it is.
-Epiphany.

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:31.501
Well, because they go,
"Do you want to see

00:05:31.501 --> 00:05:34.367
the birthplace of democracy
where it all began?"

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:35.834
And I go, "Yeah."

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:38.133
It's rubble. It's ruins.

00:05:38.133 --> 00:05:40.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.467
So that's what
we're headed for.

00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:44.434
We -- Democracy
worked for like 150 years.

00:05:44.434 --> 00:05:46.300
And then it was monarchy,

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.300
a great system
for thousands of years.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.901
And then we decided to unearth
this failed rubble system.

00:05:51.901 --> 00:05:52.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.901 --> 00:05:54.133
You know?
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:56.434
-We had the perfect
setup in 1775,

00:05:56.434 --> 00:05:57.567
a remote king.

00:05:57.567 --> 00:05:58.601
That's the best
form of government.

00:05:58.601 --> 00:05:59.601
-Yeah.

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:00.734
-He didn't know
what we're doing.

00:06:00.734 --> 00:06:01.734
We did whatever
we wanted over here.

00:06:01.734 --> 00:06:03.167
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:06:03.167 --> 00:06:04.968
But he couldn't -- It'd take
him 40 days to get over here.

00:06:04.968 --> 00:06:08.334
But we could use his name.
We'd use his name.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.033
If Spain
and Portugal step to us,

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:12.601
we're like, "King George
might not like that."

00:06:12.601 --> 00:06:14.868
We gave it all away
for stamps and tea.

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:16.667
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:17.934
When's the last time
anyone used...?

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:19.334
It's e-mail
and coffee over here.

00:06:19.334 --> 00:06:20.634
You know.

00:06:20.634 --> 00:06:22.501
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.501 --> 00:06:24.334
-You feel like humor
has changed from --

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:27.133
from when you were growing up,
like the tenor of humor?

00:06:27.133 --> 00:06:28.400
Well, when we
were little kids,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.400
I mean, I remember
as a little kid,

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.801
that's why when people say
you got to make everybody laugh,

00:06:32.801 --> 00:06:34.834
you don't have to make
young kids laugh.

00:06:34.834 --> 00:06:36.334
I don't want to make
young kids laugh.

00:06:36.334 --> 00:06:38.701
I don't have the same sense
of you because I know how dumb

00:06:38.701 --> 00:06:40.434
what I thought was funny
when I was young.

00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:44.300
I remember some kid was like,
"Hey, that guy's a real master."

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.501
And he goes, "bater!"
And I was like, at the time.

00:06:47.501 --> 00:06:49.033
I go, That's pretty.
That's pretty good.

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:50.200
Yeah, yeah.
He put...

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:51.267
[ Laughter ]

00:06:51.267 --> 00:06:53.534
That he put them
together like that.

00:06:53.534 --> 00:06:56.734
I'll give you a test.
Seth spell "I-cup."

00:06:56.734 --> 00:06:59.334
-I C-U-P.
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:59.334 --> 00:07:00.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:00.601 --> 00:07:01.601
[ Drum roll ]

00:07:01.601 --> 00:07:03.801
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.801 --> 00:07:05.601
-That's the kind of stuff.
-Yeah, So you're saying that --

00:07:05.601 --> 00:07:06.701
-We get when going back.
-In those days,

00:07:06.701 --> 00:07:08.200
-You're not judging them
for enjoying that,

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.367
but you don't want to --
You don't want

00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:10.467
to cater to that audience?

00:07:10.467 --> 00:07:12.133
-No, exactly.

00:07:12.133 --> 00:07:13.801
-And do you feel like comedy

00:07:13.801 --> 00:07:16.534
has become more negative
than when you were a kid?

00:07:16.534 --> 00:07:18.467
-Well, not just comedy.
Everything's more negative.

00:07:18.467 --> 00:07:20.567
I was watching.
Look at Taylor Swift.

00:07:20.567 --> 00:07:21.567
She goes out.

00:07:21.567 --> 00:07:22.634
[ Audience cheers ]

00:07:22.634 --> 00:07:24.434
Right?
She goes to the game.

00:07:24.434 --> 00:07:25.667
Like in the old days,
you're like,

00:07:25.667 --> 00:07:27.067
"Oh, look, an athlete
and a singer going out.

00:07:27.067 --> 00:07:28.868
This is great."
Everybody's like, "Oh!"

00:07:28.868 --> 00:07:31.367
You know, we don't even
know why everybody's upset,

00:07:31.367 --> 00:07:32.701
But they're like,
I don't like it.

00:07:32.701 --> 00:07:35.400
We're all waiting for the whole
thing to crash and burn.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.934
-Yeah.
Every game you're like,

00:07:36.934 --> 00:07:39.234
"Look at her in the stands
with the mother."

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:41.133
It's the most whole--
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:41.133 --> 00:07:45.033
-it's the most wholesome thing
I've ever seen in my life.

00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:47.968
-I'm also shocked that
sports fans are upset

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:49.701
that there's not
more focus on football.

00:07:49.701 --> 00:07:52.834
There's so little football
in a football game,

00:07:52.834 --> 00:07:54.634
and it's just those
two guys in a booth.

00:07:54.634 --> 00:07:56.133
They're trying
to make small talk.

00:07:56.133 --> 00:07:57.400
They're trying to make --
-Yes!

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.467
-They got nothing to say.
-That's right.

00:07:59.467 --> 00:08:01.234
-I think it's such
a great relief

00:08:01.234 --> 00:08:03.334
just to cut to people
who seem happy.

00:08:03.334 --> 00:08:05.467
-They must love it.
Yes, they must love it.

00:08:05.467 --> 00:08:06.868
-Yeah.
-As a Jets fan, were you --

00:08:06.868 --> 00:08:08.267
How do you feel
about the season so far?

00:08:08.267 --> 00:08:09.767
-Well, we're excited because,

00:08:09.767 --> 00:08:11.634
first of all,
people don't understand.

00:08:11.634 --> 00:08:13.434
I've been a Jets fan
since I was a little kid.

00:08:13.434 --> 00:08:15.534
The Jets, I was there
when they, well, not there,

00:08:15.534 --> 00:08:17.434
but I was watching or listening.

00:08:17.434 --> 00:08:18.634
I forget how it
was in those days.

00:08:18.634 --> 00:08:20.667
But when the Jets,
when Joe Namath was there,

00:08:20.667 --> 00:08:22.567
and I remember thinking
even as a 10-year-old kid

00:08:22.567 --> 00:08:23.834
like, "This makes sense."

00:08:23.834 --> 00:08:25.434
It's New York,
it's a big market.

00:08:25.434 --> 00:08:27.934
It'll probably be
like this forever, right?

00:08:27.934 --> 00:08:29.601
Little did I know.

00:08:29.601 --> 00:08:32.167
But when Aaron Rodgers
got the Achilles,

00:08:32.167 --> 00:08:35.167
the first set of downs,
any real Jets fan was not like,

00:08:35.167 --> 00:08:36.868
"I can't believe it."
Of course.

00:08:36.868 --> 00:08:38.133
What else would you
expect to happen?

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:39.133
Right.

00:08:39.133 --> 00:08:40.267
[ Laughter ]

00:08:40.267 --> 00:08:41.634
-So you weren't
that surprised?

00:08:41.634 --> 00:08:45.901
-No, I wasn't at all.
I mean, it made perfect sense.

00:08:45.901 --> 00:08:47.367
-We're fellow writers.
Writer strike's over.

00:08:47.367 --> 00:08:48.434
That's a good thing.

00:08:48.434 --> 00:08:50.367
You, um, you have
a negotiating tactic,

00:08:50.367 --> 00:08:51.501
because I know a lot
of people were --

00:08:51.501 --> 00:08:52.601
had their notes about like,

00:08:52.601 --> 00:08:53.901
"Oh, if I was on
the negotiating committee,

00:08:53.901 --> 00:08:55.167
I would have say this or that."

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:57.367
But you -- you take care
of your own contract.

00:08:57.367 --> 00:09:00.334
-Two months before
the writer's strike,

00:09:00.334 --> 00:09:01.567
I happen to be telling my wife,

00:09:01.567 --> 00:09:03.267
who, you know,
deals with that kind of stuff,

00:09:03.267 --> 00:09:05.133
I go when I --
All my career,

00:09:05.133 --> 00:09:07.667
a little fun secret fact
I thought about me,

00:09:07.667 --> 00:09:10.067
I go all my whole career
whenever they hand me

00:09:10.067 --> 00:09:11.767
the contract,
this started in the '80s,

00:09:11.767 --> 00:09:14.968
and it says in perpetuity
in all forms existing

00:09:14.968 --> 00:09:16.067
and in the future.

00:09:16.067 --> 00:09:17.267
Back in the '80s,

00:09:17.267 --> 00:09:19.133
this one is one of
the big contract points,

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:22.167
but I always cross it off
in every contract

00:09:22.167 --> 00:09:23.901
anyone ever handed me,
if it was on the show.

00:09:23.901 --> 00:09:26.033
Anyway, I cross it off
with my pen.

00:09:26.033 --> 00:09:27.467
So I told her that

00:09:27.467 --> 00:09:30.467
and she literally spent
a minute laughing in my face.

00:09:30.467 --> 00:09:31.767
[ Laughter ]

00:09:31.767 --> 00:09:33.968
-So in perpetuity, you were
just never comfortable

00:09:33.968 --> 00:09:36.033
with people
owning it forever.

00:09:36.033 --> 00:09:38.200
-Yeah, in all forms,
existing and in the future.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:39.567
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:09:39.567 --> 00:09:41.367
-So what would you -- what
would you negotiate instead?

00:09:41.367 --> 00:09:44.834
100 years?

00:09:44.834 --> 00:09:46.567
Well, just --
I don't like these...

00:09:46.567 --> 00:09:48.467
Well, who knew?
This is before any --

00:09:48.467 --> 00:09:50.801
This is before the streaming,
Anything like that.

00:09:50.801 --> 00:09:52.100
You know, AI.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.667
I was ahead of the game
is what I'm trying to say.

00:09:53.667 --> 00:09:55.167
-Yeah.

00:09:55.167 --> 00:09:56.567
And she laughs at you.
You're ahead of the game.

00:09:56.567 --> 00:09:58.701
-She laughed at me.
-A futurist like yourself?

00:09:58.701 --> 00:10:00.467
-Yeah, exactly.
-[ Laughs ]

00:10:00.467 --> 00:10:03.067
-Exactly.
-One final thing.

00:10:03.067 --> 00:10:06.234
You know, obviously you interact
with showbiz people.

00:10:06.234 --> 00:10:07.601
There's a lot of showbiz
small talk.

00:10:07.601 --> 00:10:08.934
Sometimes people will say,

00:10:08.934 --> 00:10:10.334
"We should get together
sometime."

00:10:10.334 --> 00:10:11.834
-Yeah.
Are you the kind of person

00:10:11.834 --> 00:10:13.667
who likes to make plans
like that?

00:10:13.667 --> 00:10:14.968
-Well, I don't.
I hate it.

00:10:14.968 --> 00:10:17.767
but I always say,
"Yes, absolutely."

00:10:17.767 --> 00:10:19.267
-Okay.
So that would be your advice.

00:10:19.267 --> 00:10:20.534
-Never show up.

00:10:20.534 --> 00:10:22.467
And then some people
actually have the nerve to,

00:10:22.467 --> 00:10:23.767
like, call you on like,

00:10:23.767 --> 00:10:24.901
"Hey, I thought we're getting
together for lunch."

00:10:24.901 --> 00:10:26.968
I was like, "Um,
what are you talking about?"

00:10:26.968 --> 00:10:28.434
[ Laughter ]

00:10:28.434 --> 00:10:29.567
Did you seriously believe

00:10:29.567 --> 00:10:30.968
we were going to get
together for lunch?

00:10:30.968 --> 00:10:33.567
[ Laughter ]

00:10:33.567 --> 00:10:35.000
So weird to me,
you know,

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.701
-So -- Right.
So it seems you --

00:10:36.701 --> 00:10:37.934
It's just like words.

00:10:37.934 --> 00:10:39.167
Like if someone's like,
"We should get together."

00:10:39.167 --> 00:10:40.601
And you're like,
"Yeah, absolutely."

00:10:40.601 --> 00:10:42.133
That's just like a pleasant way
of ending a conversation.

00:10:42.133 --> 00:10:43.567
-Well, if I say it,

00:10:43.567 --> 00:10:44.901
then I'm insulted
if they don't want to do it.

00:10:44.901 --> 00:10:46.167
-Sure, sure.

00:10:46.167 --> 00:10:47.367
That's a totally different --

00:10:47.367 --> 00:10:48.434
-That's a different dynamic.

00:10:48.434 --> 00:10:49.534
-Hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:10:49.534 --> 00:10:50.534
It's always a pleasure
to talk to you,

00:10:50.534 --> 00:10:51.734
Colin Quinn.

00:10:51.734 --> 00:10:53.634
For ticket information
go to colinquinn.com.

